and diagonal of G^-1.\n1929 | \n1930 |         Used when we have an SVD decomposition of X\n1931 |         (n_samples > n_features and X is dense).\n1932 |         \"\"\"\n1933 |         w = ((singvals_sq + alpha) ** -1) - (alpha**-1)\n1934 |         if self.fit_intercept:\n1935 |             # detect intercept column\n1936 |             normalized_sw = sqrt_sw / np.linalg.norm(sqrt_sw)\n1937 |             intercept_dim = _find_smallest_angle(normalized_sw, U)\n1938 |             # cancel the regularization for the intercept\n1939 |             w[intercept_dim] = -(alpha**-1)\n1940 |         c = np.dot(U, self._diag_dot(w, UT_y)) + (alpha**-1) * y\n1941 |         G_inverse_diag = self._decomp_diag(w, U) + (alpha**-1)\n1942 |         if len(y.shape) != 1:\n1943 |             # handle case where y is 2-d\n1944 |             G_inverse_diag = G_inverse_diag[:, np.newaxis]\n1945 |         return G_inverse_diag, c\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/random_projection.py:_gaussian_random_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n162 | def _gaussian_random_matrix(n_components, n_features, random_state=None):\n163 |     \"\"\"Generate a dense Gaussian random matrix.\n164 | \n165 |     The components of the random matrix are drawn from\n166 | \n167 |         N(0, 1.0 / n_components).\n168 | \n169 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <gaussian_random_matrix>`.\n170 | \n171 |     Parameters\n172 |     ----------\n173 |     n_components : int,\n174 |         Dimensionality of the target projection space.\n175 | \n176 |     n_features : int,\n177 |         Dimensionality of the original source space.\n178 | \n179 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n180 |         Controls the pseudo random number generator used to generate the matrix\n181 |         at fit time.\n182 |         Pass an int for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n183 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n184 | \n185 |     Returns\