ntially\n 79 |         increasing the number of iterations of the projected gradient\n 80 |         procedure. 0.01 is a commonly used value in the optimization\n 81 |         literature.\n 82 |     beta : float\n 83 |         Factor by which the step size is decreased (resp. increased) until\n 84 |         (resp. as long as) the sufficient decrease condition is satisfied.\n 85 |         Larger values allow to find a better step size but lead to longer line\n 86 |         search. 0.1 is a commonly used value in the optimization literature.\n 87 |     Returns\n 88 |     -------\n 89 |     H : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 90 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 91 |     grad : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 92 |         The gradient.\n 93 |     n_iter : int\n 94 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n 95 |     References\n 96 |     ----------\n 97 |     C.-J. Lin. Projected gradient methods for non-negative matrix\n 98 |     factorization. Neural Computation, 19(2007), 2756-2779.\n 99 |     https://www.csie.ntu.edu.tw/~cjlin/nmf/\n100 |     \"\"\"\n101 |     WtX = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n102 |     WtW = np.dot(W.T, W)\n103 | \n104 |     # values justified in the paper (alpha is renamed gamma)\n105 |     gamma = 1\n106 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n107 |         grad = np.dot(WtW, H) - WtX\n108 |         if alpha > 0 and l1_ratio == 1.0:\n109 |             grad += alpha\n110 |         elif alpha > 0:\n111 |             grad += alpha * (l1_ratio + (1 - l1_ratio) * H)\n112 | \n113 |         # The following multiplication with a boolean array is more than twice\n114 |         # as fast as indexing into grad.\n115 |         if _norm(grad * np.logical_or(grad < 0, H > 0)) < tol:\n116 |             break\n117 | \n118 |         Hp = H\n119 | \n120 |         for inner_iter in range(20):\n121 |             # Gradient step.\n122 |             Hn = H - gamma * grad\n123 |             # Projection step.\n124 |             Hn *= Hn > 0\n125 |             d = Hn - H\n126 |             gradd = np.dot(grad.ravel(), d.ravel())\n127 |             dQd = np.dot(np.dot(WtW, d).ravel(), d.ravel())\n128 |             suff_decr = (1 - sigma) * gradd + 0.5 * dQd < 0\n129 |             if inner_iter == 0:\n130 |                 decr_gamma = not suff_decr\n131 | \n132 |             if decr_gamma:\n133 |                 if suff_decr:\n134 |                     H = Hn\n135 |                     break\n136 |                 else:\n137 |                     gamma *= beta\n138 |             elif not suff_decr or (Hp == Hn).all():\n139 |                 H = Hp\n140 |                 break\n141 |             else:\n142 |                 gamma /= beta\n143 |                 Hp = Hn\n144 | \n145 |     if n_iter == max_iter:\n146 |         warnings.warn(\"Iteration limit reached in nls subproblem.\", ConvergenceWarning)\n147 | \n148 |     return H, grad, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1918 | def _generate_get_feature_names_out(estimator, n_features_out, input_features=None):\n1919 |     \"\"\"Generate feature names out for estimator using the estimator name as the prefix.\n1920 | \n1921 |     The input_feature names are validated but not used. This function is useful\n1922 |     for estimators that generate their own names based on `n_features_out`, i.e. PCA.\n1923 | \n1924 |     Parameters\n1925 |     ----------\n1926 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1927 |         Estimator producing output feature names.\n1928 | \n1929 |     n_feature_out : int\n1930 |         Number of feature names out.\n1931 | \n1932 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n1933 |         Only used to validate feature names with `estimator.feature_names_in_`.\n1934 | \n1935 |     Returns\n1936 |     -------\n1937 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n1938 |         Feature names in.\n1939 |     \"\"\"\n1940 |     _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features, generate_names=False)\n1941 |     estimator_name = estimator.__class__.__name__.lower()\n1942 |     return np.asarray(\n1943 |         [f\"{estimator_name}{i}\" for i in range(n_features_out)], dtype=object\n1944 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n634 | def fit(self, X, Y):\n635 |         \"\"\"Fit model to data.\n636 | \n637 |         Parameters\n638 |         ----------\n639 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n640 |             Training vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n641 |             `n_features` is the number of predictors.\n642 | \n643 |         Y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_targets)\n644 |             Target vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n645 |             `n_targets` is the number of response variables.\n646 | \n647 |         Returns\n648 |         -------\n649 |         self : object\n650 |             Fitted model.\n651 |         \"\"\"\n652 |         super().fit(X, Y)\n653 |         # expose the fitted attributes `x_scores_` and `y_scores_`\n654 |         self.x_scores_ = self._x_scores\n655 |         self.y_scores_ = self._y_scores\n656 |         return self\n```\n\nFound file `benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py` for the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me get more details about the NMF implementation:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['non_negative_factorization', 'multiplicative_update']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/datasets/_arff_parser.py:_sparse_data_to_array\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD\n##Searching for term \"non_negative_factorization\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `non_negative_factorization`.\n```\n 874 | def non_negative_factorization(\n 875 |     X,\n 876 |     W=None,\n 877 |     H=None,\n 878 |     n_components=None,\n 879 |     *,\n 880 |     init=None,\n 881 |     update_H=True,\n 882 |     solver=\"cd\",\n 883 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 884 |     tol=1e-4,\n 885 |     max_iter=200,\n 886 |     alpha=\"deprecated\",\n 887 |     alpha_W=0.0,\n 888 |     alpha_H=\"same\",\n 889 |     l1_ratio=0.0,\n 890 |     regularization=\"deprecated\",\n 891 |     random_state=None,\n 892 |     verbose=0,\n 893 |     shuffle=False,\n 894 | ):\n 895 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n 896 | \n 897 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n 898 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"multiplicative_update\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_multiplicative_update_w`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n537 | def _multiplicative_update_w(\n538 |     X,\n539 |     W,\n540 |     H,\n541 |     beta_loss,\n542 |     l1_reg_W,\n543 |     l2_reg_W,\n544 |     gamma,\n545 |     H_sum=None,\n546 |     HHt=None,\n547 |     XHt=None,\n548 |     update_H=True,\n549 | ):\n550 |     \"\"\"Update W in Multiplicative Update NMF.\"\"\"\n551 |     if beta_loss == 2:\n552 |         # Numerator\n553 |         if XHt is None:\n554 |             XHt = safe_sparse_dot(X, H.T)\n555 |         if update_H:\n556 |             # avoid a copy of XHt, which will be re-computed (update_H=True)\n557 |             numerator = XHt\n558 |         else:\n559 |             # preserve the XHt, which is not re-computed (update_H=False)\n560 |             numerator = XHt.copy()\n561 | \n562 |         # Denominator\n563 |         if HHt is None:\n564 |             HHt = np.dot(H, H.T)\n565 |         denominator = np.dot(W, HHt)\n566 | \n567 |     else:\n568 |         # Numerator\n569 |         # if X is sparse, compute WH only where X is non zero\n570 |         WH_safe_X = _special_sparse_dot(W, H, X)\n571 |         if sp.issparse(X):\n572 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X.data\n573 |             X_data = X.data\n574 |         else:\n575 |             WH_safe_X_data = WH_safe_X\n576 |             X_data = X\n577 |             # copy used in the Denominator\n578 |             WH = WH_safe_X.copy()\n579 |             if beta_loss - 1.0 < 0:\n580 |                 WH[WH == 0] = EPSILON\n581 | \n582 |         # to avoid taking a negative power of zero\n583 |         if beta_loss - 2.0 < 0:\n584 |             WH_safe_X_data[WH_safe_X_data == 0] = EPSILON\n585 | \n586 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n587 |             np.divide(X_data, WH_safe_X_data, out=WH_safe_X_data)\n588 |         elif beta_loss == 0:\n589 |             # speeds up computation time\n590 |             # refer to /numpy/numpy/issues/9363\n591 |             WH_safe_X_data **= -1\n592 |             WH_safe_X_data **= 2\n593 |             # element-wise multiplication\n594 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n595 |         else:\n596 |             WH_safe_X_data **= beta_loss - 2\n597 |             # element-wise multiplication\n598 |             WH_safe_X_data *= X_data\n599 | \n600 |         # here numerator = dot(X * (dot(W, H) ** (beta_loss - 2)), H.T)\n601 |         numerator = safe_sparse_dot(WH_safe_X, H.T)\n602 | \n603 |         # Denominator\n604 |         if beta_loss == 1:\n605 |             if H_sum is None:\n606 |                 H_sum = np.sum(H, axis=1)  # shape(n_components, )\n607 |             denominator = H_sum[np.newaxis, :]\n608 | \n609 |         else:\n610 |             # computation of WHHt = dot(dot(W, H) ** beta_loss - 1, H.T)\n611 |             if sp.issparse(X):\n612 |                 # memory efficient computation\n613 |                 # (compute row by row, avoiding the dense matrix WH)\n614 |                 WHHt = np.empty(W.shape)\n615 |                 for i in range(X.shape[0]):\n616 |                     WHi = np.dot(W[i, :], H)\n617 |                     if beta_loss - 1 < 0:\n618 |                         WHi[WHi == 0] = EPSILON\n619 |                     WHi **= beta_loss - 1\n620 |                     WHHt[i, :] = np.dot(WHi, H.T)\n621 |             else:\n622 |                 WH **= beta_loss - 1\n623 |                 WHHt = np.dot(WH, H.T)\n624 |             denominator = WHHt\n625 | \n626 |     # Add L1 and L2 regularization\n627 |     if l1_reg_W > 0:\n628 |         denominator += l1_reg_W\n629 |     if l2_reg_W > 0:\n630 |         denominator = denominator + l2_reg_W * W\n631 |     denominator[denominator == 0] = EPSILON\n632 | \n633 |     numerator /= denominator\n634 |     delta_W = numerator\n635 | \n636 |     # gamma is in ]0, 1]\n637 |     if gamma != 1:\n638 |         delta_W **= gamma\n639 | \n640 |     return delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n725 | def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n726 |     X,\n727 |     W,\n728 |     H,\n729 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n730 |     max_iter=200,\n731 |     tol=1e-4,\n732 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n733 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n734 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n735 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n736 |     update_H=True,\n737 |     verbose=0,\n738 | ):\n739 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization with Multiplicative Update.\n740 | \n741 |     The objective function is _beta_divergence(X, WH) and is minimized with an\n742 |     alternating minimization of W and H. Each minimization is done with a\n743 |     Multiplicative Update.\n744 | \n745 |     Parameters\n746 |     ----------\n747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n748 |         Constant input matrix.\n749 | \n750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n752 | \n753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n755 | \n756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n764 | \n765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n766 |         Number of iterations.\n767 | \n768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n770 | \n771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n773 | \n774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n775 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n776 | \n777 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n778 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n779 | \n780 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n781 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n782 | \n783 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n784 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n785 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n786 | \n787 |     verbose : int, default=0\n788 |         The verbosity level.\n789 | \n790 |     Returns\n791 |     -------\n792 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n793 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n794 | \n795 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n796 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n797 | \n798 |     n_iter : int\n799 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n800 | \n801 |     References\n802 |     ----------\n803 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n804 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n805 |     \"\"\"\n806 |     start_time = time.time()\n807 | \n808 |     beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss)\n809 | \n810 |     # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n811 |     if beta_loss < 1:\n812 |         gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - beta_loss)\n813 |     elif beta_loss > 2:\n814 |         gamma = 1.0 / (beta_loss - 1.0)\n815 |     else:\n816 |         gamma = 1.0\n817 | \n818 |     # used for the convergence criterion\n819 |     error_at_init = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n820 |     previous_error = error_at_init\n821 | \n822 |     H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n823 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n824 |         # update W\n825 |         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n826 |         delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n827 |             X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n828 |         )\n829 |         W *= delta_W\n830 | \n831 |         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n832 |         if beta_loss < 1:\n833 |             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n834 | \n835 |         # update H\n836 |         if update_H:\n837 |             delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n838 |                 X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n839 |             )\n840 |             H *= delta_H\n841 | \n842 |             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n843 |             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n844 | \n845 |             # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n846 |             if beta_loss <= 1:\n847 |                 H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n848 | \n849 |         # test convergence criterion every 10 iterations\n850 |         if tol > 0 and n_iter % 10 == 0:\n851 |             error = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n852 | \n853 |             if verbose:\n854 |                 iter_time = time.time()\n855 |                 print(\n856 |                     \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds, error: %f\"\n857 |                     % (n_iter, iter_time - start_time, error)\n858 |                 )\n859 | \n860 |             if (previous_error - error) / error_at_init < tol:\n861 |                 break\n862 |             previous_error = error\n863 | \n864 |     # do not print if we have already printed in the convergence test\n865 |     if verbose and (tol == 0 or n_iter % 10 != 0):\n866 |         end_time = time.time()\n867 |         print(\n868 |             \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds.\" % (n_iter, end_time - start_time)\n869 |         )\n870 | \n871 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R langu