l}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `calibration_curve`.\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot_calibration\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot_calibration`.\nJust show the structure of this file due to response length limitations:\n```\n\n\nn_samples = 50000\nn_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n\n# Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n# half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n# blob is therefore 0.5.\ncenters = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\nX, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n                  random_state=42)\n\ny[:n_samples // 2] = 0\ny[n_samples // 2:] = 1\nsample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n\n# split train, test for calibration\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n    train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\nclf = GaussianNB()\nprob_pos_clf = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with isotonic calibration\nclf_isotonic = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='isotonic')\nprob_pos_isotonic = clf_isotonic.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with sigmoid calibration\nclf_sigmoid = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='sigmoid')\nprob_pos_sigmoid = clf_sigmoid.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\nclf_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_clf, sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_isotonic_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_isotonic,\n                                      sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_sigmoid_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_sigmoid,\n                                     sample_weight=sw_test)\ny_unique = np.unique(y)\ncolors = cm.rainbow(np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, y_unique.size))\norder = np.lexsort((prob_pos_clf, ))\n\n```\nHint: Search `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py` to get the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let's also check the metrics module since it seems relevant to visualization:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['plot', 'matplotlib']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sklearn/metrics/*.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.__init__\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot\", \"matplotlib\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 92 | def plot(self, ax=None, *, name=None, **kwargs):\n 93 |         \"\"\"Plot visualization.\n 94 | \n 95 |         Extra keyword arguments will be passed to matplotlib's `plot`.\n 96 | \n 97 |         Parameters\n 98 |         ----------\n 99 |         ax : Matplotlib Axes, default=None\n100 |             Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is\n101 |             created.\n102 | \n103 |         name : str, default=None\n104 |             Name of precision recall curve for labeling. If `None`, use the\n105 |             name of the estimator.\n106 | \n107 |         **kwargs : dict\n108 |             Keyword arguments to be passed to matplotlib's `plot`.\n109 | \n110 |         Returns\n111 |         -------\n112 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.metrics.PrecisionRecallDisplay`\n113 |             Object that stores computed values.\n114 |         \"\"\"\n115 |         check_matplotlib_support(\"PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot\")\n116 | \n117 |         name = self.estimator_name if name is None else name\n118 | \n119 |         line_kwargs = {\"drawstyle\": \"steps-post\"}\n120 |         if self.average_precision is not None and name is not None:\n121 |             line_kwargs[\"label\"] = f\"{name} (AP = {self.average_precision:0.2f})\"\n122 |         elif self.average_precision is not None:\n123 |             line_kwargs[\"label\"] = f\"AP = {self.average_precision:0.2f}\"\n124 |         elif name is not None:\n125 |             line_kwargs[\"label\"] = name\n126 |         line_kwargs.update(**kwargs)\n127 | \n128 |         import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n129 | \n130 |         if ax is None:\n131 |             fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n132 | \n133 |         (self.line_,) = ax.plot(self.recall, self.precision, **line_kwargs)\n134 |         info_pos_label = (\n135 |             f\" (Positive label: {self.pos_label})\" if self.pos_label is not None else \"\"\n136 |         )\n137 | \n138 |         xlabel = \"Recall\" + info_pos_label\n139 |         ylabel = \"Precision\" + info_pos_label\n140 |         ax.set(xlabel=xlabel, ylabel=ylabel)\n141 | \n142 |         if \"label\" in line_kwargs:\n143 |             ax.legend(loc=\"lower left\")\n144 | \n145 |         self.ax_ = ax\n146 |         self.figure_ = ax.figure\n147 |         return self\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"matplotlib\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from itertools import product\n  2 | \n  3 | import numpy as np\n  4 | \n  5 | from .. import confusion_matrix\n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | from ...utils import deprecated\n  8 | from ...utils.multiclass import unique_labels\n  9 | from ...base import is_classifier\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | class ConfusionMatrixDisplay:\n 13 |     \"\"\"Confusion Matrix visualization.\n 14 | \n 15 |     It is recommend to use\n 16 |     :func:`~sklearn.metrics.ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_estimator` or\n 17 |     :func:`~sklearn.metrics.ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions` to\n 18 |     create a :class:`ConfusionMatrixDisplay`. All parameters are stored as\n 19 |     attributes.\n 20 | \n 21 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 22 | \n 23 |     Parameters\n 24 |     ----------\n 25 |     confusion_matrix : ndarray of shape (n_classes, n_classes)\n 26 |         Confusion matrix.\n 27 | \n 28 |     display_labels : ndarray of shape (n_classes,), default=None\n 29 |         Display labels for plot. If None, display labels are set from 0 to\n 30 |         `n_classes - 1`.\n 31 | \n 32 |     Attributes\n 33 |     ----------\n 34 |     im_ : matplotlib AxesImage\n 35 |         Image representing the confusion matrix.\n 36 | \n 37 |     text_ : ndarray of shape (n_classes, n_classes), dtype=matplotlib Text, \\\n 38 |             or None\n 39 |         Array of matplotlib axes. `None` if `include_values` is false.\n 40 | \n 41 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 42 |         Axes with confusion matrix.\n 43 | \n 44 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 45 |         Figure containing the confusion matrix.\n 46 | \n 47 |     See Also\n 48 |     --------\n 49 |     confusion_matrix : Compute Confusion Matrix to evaluate the accuracy of a\n 50 |         classification.\n 51 |     ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_estimator : Plot the confusion matrix\n 52 |         given an estimator, the data, and the label.\n 53 |     ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions : Plot the confusion matrix\n 54 |         given the true and predicted labels.\n 55 | \n 56 |     Examples\n 57 |     --------\n 58 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 60 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y,\n 65 |     ...                                                     random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0)\n 67 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 68 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n 69 |     >>> predictions = clf.predict(X_test)\n 70 |     >>> cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, predictions, labels=clf.classes_)\n 71 |     >>> disp = ConfusionMatrixDisplay(confusion_matrix=cm,\n 72 |     ...                               display_labels=clf.classes_)\n 73 |     >>> disp.plot()\n 74 |     <...>\n 75 |     >>> plt.show()\n 76 |     \"\"\"\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from .base import _get_response\n  2 | \n  3 | from .. import average_precision_score\n  4 | from .. import precision_recall_curve\n  5 | \n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | \n  8 | \n  9 | class PrecisionRecallDisplay:\n 10 |     \"\"\"Precision Recall visualization.\n 11 | \n 12 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.plot_precision_recall_curve`\n 13 |     to create a visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 14 | \n 15 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 16 | \n 17 |     Parameters\n 18 |     -----------\n 19 |     precision : ndarray\n 20 |         Precision values.\n 21 | \n 22 |     recall : ndarray\n 23 |         Recall values.\n 24 | \n 25 |     average_precision : float, default=None\n 26 |         Average precision. If None, the average precision is not shown.\n 27 | \n 28 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 29 |         Name of estimator. If None, then the estimator name is not shown.\n 30 | \n 31 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 32 |         The class considered as the positive class. If None, the class will not\n 33 |         be shown in the legend.\n 34 | \n 35 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 36 | \n 37 |     Attributes\n 38 |     ----------\n 39 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 40 |         Precision recall curve.\n 41 | \n 42 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 43 |         Axes with precision recall curve.\n 44 | \n 45 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 46 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 47 | \n 48 |     See Also\n 49 |     --------\n 50 |     precision_recall_curve : Compute precision-recall pairs for different\n 51 |         probability thresholds.\n 52 |     plot_precision_recall_curve : Plot Precision Recall Curve for binary\n 53 |         classifiers.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Examples\n 56 |     --------\n 57 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import (precision_recall_curve,\n 60 |     ...                              PrecisionRecallDisplay)\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y,\n 65 |     ...                                                     random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0)\n 67 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 68 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n 69 |     >>> predictions = clf.predict(X_test)\n 70 |     >>> precision, recall, _ = precision_recall_curve(y_test, predictions)\n 71 |     >>> disp = PrecisionRecallDisplay(precision=precision, recall=recall)\n 72 |     >>> disp.plot()\n 73 |     <...>\n 74 |     >>> plt.show()\n 75 |     \"\"\"\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/__init__.py:check_matplotlib_support`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1085 | def check_matplotlib_support(caller_name):\n1086 |     \"\"\"Raise ImportError with detailed error message if mpl is not installed.\n1087 | \n1088 |     Plot utilities like :func:`plot_partial_dependence` should lazily import\n1089 |     matplotlib and call this helper before any computation.\n1090 | \n1091 |     Parameters\n1092 |     ----------\n1093 |     caller_name : str\n1094 |         The name of the caller that requires matplotlib.\n1095 |     \"\"\"\n1096 |     try:\n1097 |         import matplotlib  # noqa\n1098 |