2500\u2500 doc/tutorial\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/sphinxext\n"}, {"content": "Let me look for the `fetch_openml` implementation since it's mentioned in the issue.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['fetch_openml']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"fetch_openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `fetch_openml`.\n```\n664 | def fetch_openml(\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check for the data download and validation related functions:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_download_data_to_bunch', '_get_data_description_by_id', '_get_data_info_by_name']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_download_data_to_bunch\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_get_data_description_by_id\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_get_data_description_by_id`.\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_get_data_info_by_name\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_info_by_name`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_get_data_info_by_name`.\n```\n364 | def _get_data_info_by_name(\n365 |     name: str, version: Union[int, str], data_home: Optional[str]\n366 | ):\n367 |     \"\"\"\n368 |     Utilizes the openml dataset listing api to find a dataset by\n369 |     name/version\n370 |     OpenML api function:\n371 |     https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_list_data_name_data_name\n372 | \n373 |     Parameters\n374 |     ----------\n375 |     name : str\n376 |         name of the dataset\n377 | \n378 |     version : int or str\n379 |         If version is an integer, the exact name/version will be obtained from\n380 |         OpenML. If version is a string (value: \"active\") it will take the first\n381 |         version from OpenML that is annotated as active. Any other string\n382 |         values except \"active\" are treated as integer.\n383 | \n384 |     data_home : str or None\n385 |         Location to cache the response. None if no cache is required.\n386 | \n387 |     Returns\n388 |     -------\n389 |     first_dataset : json\n390 |         json representation of the first dataset object that adhired to the\n391 |         search criteria\n392 | \n393 |     \"\"\"\n394 |     if version == \"active\":\n395 |         # situation in which we return the oldest active version\n396 |         url = _SEARCH_NAME.format(name) + \"/status/active/\"\n397 |         error_msg = \"No active dataset {} found.\".format(name)\n398 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n399 |             url, error_msg, data_home=data_home\n400 |         )\n401 |         res = json_data['data']['dataset']\n402 |         if len(res) > 1:\n403 |             warn(\"Multiple active versions of the dataset matching the name\"\n404 |                  \" {name} exist. Versions may be fundamentally different, \"\n405 |                  \"returning version\"\n406 |                  \" {version}.\".format(name=name, version=res[0]['version']))\n407 |         return res[0]\n408 | \n409 |     # an integer version has been provided\n410 |     url = (_SEARCH_NAME + \"/data_version/{}\").format(name, version)\n411 |     try:\n412 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n413 |             url, error_message=None, data_home=data_home\n414 |         )\n415 |     except OpenMLError:\n416 |         # we can do this in 1 function call if OpenML does not require the\n417 |         # specification of the dataset status (i.e., return datasets with a\n418 |         # given name / version regardless of active, deactivated, etc. )\n419 |         # TODO: feature request OpenML.\n420 |         url += \"/status/deactivated\"\n421 |         error_msg = \"Dataset {} with version {} not found.\".format(name,\n422 |                                                                    version)\n423 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n424 |             url, error_msg, data_home=data_home\n425 |         )\n426 | \n427 |     return json_data['data']['dataset'][0]\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check for the data loading functions:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_load_arff_response', '_convert_arff_data', '_convert_arff_data_dataframe']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_convert_arff_data\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_convert_arff_data`.\n```\n233 | def _convert_arff_data(\n234 |     arff: ArffContainerType,\n235 |     col_slice_x: List[int],\n236 |     col_slice_y: List[int],\n237 |     shape: Optional[Tuple] = None\n238 | ) -> Tuple:\n239 |     \"\"\"\n240 |     converts the arff object into the appropriate matrix type (np.array or\n241 |     scipy.sparse.csr_matrix) based on the 'data part' (i.e., in the\n242 |     liac-arff dict, the object from the 'data' key)\n243 | \n244 |     Parameters\n245 |     ----------\n246 |     arff : dict\n247 |         As obtained from liac-arff object.\n248 | \n249 |     col_slice_x : list\n250 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n251 |         as X data\n252 | \n253 |     col_slice_y : list\n254 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n255 |         as y data\n256 | \n257 |     Returns\n258 |     -------\n259 |     X : np.array or scipy.sparse.csr_matrix\n260 |     y : np.array\n261 |     \"\"\"\n262 |     arff_data = arff['data']\n263 |     if isinstance(arff_data, Generator):\n264 |         if shape is None:\n265 |             raise ValueError(\n266 |                 \"shape must be provided when arr['data'] is a Generator\"\n267 |             )\n268 |         if shape[0] == -1:\n269 |             count = -1\n270 |         else:\n271 |             count = shape[0] * shape[1]\n272 |         data = np.fromiter(itertools.chain.from_iterable(arff_data),\n273 |                            dtype='float64', count=count)\n274 |         data = data.reshape(*shape)\n275 |         X = data[:, col_slice_x]\n276 |         y = data[:, col_slice_y]\n277 |         return X, y\n278 |     elif isinstance(arff_data, tuple):\n279 |         arff_data_X = _split_sparse_columns(arff_data, col_slice_x)\n280 |         num_obs = max(arff_data[1]) + 1\n281 |         X_shape = (num_obs, len(col_slice_x))\n282 |         X = scipy.sparse.coo_matrix(\n283 |             (arff_data_X[0], (arff_data_X[1], arff_data_X[2])),\n284 |             shape=X_shape, dtype=np.float64)\n285 |         X = X.tocsr()\n286 |         y = _sparse_data_to_array(arff_data, col_slice_y)\n287 |         return X, y\n288 |     else:\n289 |         # This should never happen\n290 |         raise ValueError('Unexpected Data Type obtained from arff.')\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_convert_arff_data_dataframe\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data_dataframe`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_convert_arff_data_dataframe`.\n```\n310 | def _convert_arff_data_dataframe(\n311 |     arff: ArffContainerType, columns: List, features_dict: Dict[str, Any]\n312 | ) -> Tuple:\n313 |     \"\"\"Convert the ARFF object into a pandas DataFrame.\n314 | \n315 |     Parameters\n316 |     ----------\n317 |     arff : dict\n318 |         As obtained from liac-arff object.\n319 | \n320 |     columns : list\n321 |         Columns from dataframe to return.\n322 | \n323 |     features_dict : dict\n324 |         Maps feature name to feature info from openml.\n325 | \n326 |     Returns\n327 |     -------\n328 |     result : tuple\n329 |         tuple with the resulting dataframe\n330 |     \"\"\"\n331 |     pd = check_pandas_support('fetch_openml with as_frame=True')\n332 | \n333 |     attributes = OrderedDict(arff['attributes'])\n334 |     arff_columns = list(attributes)\n335 | \n336 |     if not isinstance(arff['data'], Generator):\n337 |         raise ValueError(\n338 |             \"arff['data'] must be a generator when converting to pd.DataFrame.\"\n339 |         )\n340 | \n341 |     # calculate chunksize\n342 |     first_row = next(arff['data'])\n343 |     first_df = pd.DataFrame([first_row], columns=arff_columns)\n344 | \n345 |     row_bytes = first_df.memory_usage(deep=True).sum()\n346 |     chunksize = get_chunk_n_rows(row_bytes)\n347 | \n348 |     # read arff data with chunks\n349 |     columns_to_keep = [col for col in arff_columns if col in columns]\n350 |     dfs = []\n351 |     dfs.append(first_df[columns_to_keep])\n352 |     for data in _chunk_generator(arff['data'], chunksize):\n353 |         dfs.append(pd.DataFrame(data, columns=arff_columns)[columns_to_keep])\n354 |     df = pd.concat(dfs, ignore_index=True)\n355 | \n356 |     for column in columns_to_keep:\n357 |         dtype = _feature_to_dtype(features_dict[column])\n358 |         if dtype == 'category':\n359 |             dtype = pd.api.types.CategoricalDtype(attributes[column])\n360 |         df[column] = df[column].astype(dtype, copy=False)\n361 |     return (df, )\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check for the helper functions used:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_open_openml_url', '_get_json_content_from_openml_api']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_open_openml_url\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_open_openml_url`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_open_openml_url`.\n```\n 73 | def _open_openml_url(openml_path: str, data_home: Optional[str]):\n 74 |     \"\"\"\n 75 |     Returns a resource from OpenML.org. Caches it to data_home if required.\n 76 | \n 77 |     Parameters\n 78 |     ----------\n 79 |     openml_path : str\n 80 |         OpenML URL that will be accessed. This will be prefixes with\n 81 |         _OPENML_PREFIX\n 82 | \n 83 |     data_home : str\n 84 |         Directory to which the files will be cached. If None, no caching will\n 85 |         be applied.\n 86 | \n 87 |     Returns\n 88 |     -------\n 89 |     result : stream\n 90 |         A stream to the OpenML resource\n 91 |     \"\"\"\n 92 |     def is_gzip_encoded(_fsrc):\n 93 |         return _fsrc.info().get('Content-Encoding', '') == 'gzip'\n 94 | \n 95 |     req = Request(_OPENML_PREFIX + openml_path)\n 96 |     req.add_header('Accept-encoding', 'gzip')\n 97 | \n 98 |     if data_home is None:\n 99 |         fsrc = urlopen(req)\n100 |         if is_gzip_encoded(fsrc):\n101 |             return gzip.GzipFile(fileobj=fsrc, mode='rb')\n102 |         return fsrc\n103 | \n104 |     local_path = _get_local_path(openml_path, data_home)\n105 |     if not os.path.exists(local_path):\n106 |         try:\n107 |             os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(local_path))\n108 |         except OSError:\n109 |             # potentially, the directory has been created already\n110 |             pass\n111 | \n112 |         try:\n113 |             with closing(urlopen(req)) as fsrc:\n114 |                 opener: Callable\n115 |                 if is_gzip_encoded(fsrc):\n116 |                     opener = open\n117 |                 else:\n118 |                     opener = gzip.GzipFile\n119 |                 with opener(local_path, 'wb') as fdst:\n120 |                     shutil.copyfileobj(fsrc, fdst)\n121 |         except Exception:\n122 |             if os.path.exists(local_path):\n123 |                 os.unlink(local_path)\n124 |             raise\n125 | \n126 |     # XXX: First time, decompression will not be necessary (by using fsrc), but\n127 |     # it will happen nonetheless\n128 |     return gzip.GzipFile(local_path, 'rb')\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_get_json_content_from_openml_api\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_json_content_from_openml_api`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_get_json_content_from_openml_api`.\n```\n136 | def _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n137 |     url: str,\n138 |     error_message: Optional[str],\n139 |     data_home: Optional[str]\n140 | ) -> Dict:\n141 |     \"\"\"\n142 |     Loads json data from the openml api\n143 | \n144 |     Parameters\n145 |     ----------\n146 |     url : str\n147 |         The URL to load from. Should be an official OpenML endpoint\n148 | \n149 |     error_message : str or None\n150 |         The error message to raise if an acceptable OpenML error is thrown\n151 |         (acceptable error is, e.g., data id not found. Other errors, like 404's\n152 |         will throw the native error message)\n153 | \n154 |     data_home : str or None\n155 |         Location to cache the response. None if no cache is required.\n156 | \n157 |     Returns\n158 |     -------\n159 |     json_data : json\n160 |         the json result from the OpenML server if the call was successful.\n161 |         An exception otherwise.\n162 |     \"\"\"\n163 | \n164 |     @_retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)\n165 |     def _load_json():\n166 |         with closing(_open_openml_url(url, data_home)) as response:\n167 |             return json.loads(response.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n168 | \n169 |     try:\n170 |         return _load_json()\n171 |     except HTTPError as error:\n172 |         # 412 is an OpenML specific error code, indicating a generic error\n173 |         # (e.g., data not found)\n174 |         if error.code != 412:\n175 |             raise error\n176 | \n177 |     # 412 error, not in except for nicer traceback\n178 |     raise OpenMLError(error_message)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the issue and the codebase, I can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement MD5 checksum validation for downloaded ARFF files. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430\nline: 436\nfunction: _get_data_description_by_id\nclass: N/A\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 509\nline: 544\nline: 613\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\nclass: N/A\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491\nline: 503\nline: 505\nfunction: _load_arff_response\nclass: N/A\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 233\nline: 238\nfunction: _convert_arff_data\nclass: N/A\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 73\nline: 92\nline: 95\nfunction: _open_openml_url\nclass: N/A\n```\n\nThese locations are critical because:\n1. `_get_data_description_by_id` is responsible for fetching the dataset metadata including the md5_checksum\n2. `_download_data_to_bunch` handles the actual data download and needs to be modified to include checksum validation\n3. `_load_arff_response` loads the data from the URL\n4. `_convert_arff_data` processes the loaded data\n5. `_open_openml_url` handles the URL loading and caching mechanisms\n\nThe core modifications would need to be made in `_download_data_to_bunch` to include the checksum validation logic. This function already has access to the dataset description through the `data_description` parameter, so adding the MD5 checksum validation would be straightforward.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 136964, "completion_tokens": 661}, "time": 93.6782329082489}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30128", "found_files": [["sklearn/metrics/_regression.py", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py", "sklearn/metrics/__init__.py"]], "found_modules": [["examples/gaussian_process/plot_gpr_on_structured_data.py:SequenceKernel", "doc/sphinxext/dropdown_anchors.py:setup", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/_structures.py:NegativeInfinityType", "sklearn/utils/_mocking.py:MockDataFrame", "benchmarks/bench_tsne_mnist.py:sanitize", "doc/conftest.py:setup_grid_search", "examples/linear_model/plot_sgd_iris.py:plot_hyperplane", "examples/cluster/plot_adjusted_for_chance_measures.py:random_labels", "sklearn/utils/fixes.py:_object_dtype_isnan", "benchmarks/bench_sparsify.py:benchmark_dense_predict", "doc/sphinxext/sphinx_issues.py:IssueRole", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/decomposition.py:MiniBatchDictionaryLearningBenchmark", "benchmarks/bench_sparsify.py:score", "examples/ensemble/plot_adaboost_multiclass.py:misclassification_error", "sklearn/utils/parallel.py:delayed", "examples/applications/plot_out_of_core_classification.py:ReutersParser", "doc/conftest.py:skip_if_cupy_not_installed", "examples/ensemble/plot_feature_transformation.py:gbdt_apply", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/common.py:Estimator", "sklearn/metrics/_regression.py:_check_reg_targets", "sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:_argmin_reduce", "sklearn/utils/_metadata_requests.py:MetadataRequest", "sklearn/ensemble/_hist_gradient_boosting/grower.py:TreeGrower", "examples/miscellaneous/plot_metadata_routing.py:ExampleRegressor", "doc/conf.py:reset_sklearn_config", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_estimator_name", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_find_matching_floating_dtype"]], "found_entities": [["examples/gaussian_process/plot_gpr_on_structured_data.py:SequenceKernel.is_stationary", "doc/sphinxext/dropdown_anchors.py:setup", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/_structures.py:NegativeInfinityType.__repr__", "sklearn/utils/_mocking.py:MockDataFrame.take", "benchmarks/bench_tsne_mnist.py:sanitize", "doc/con