create_subplots`.\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n153 |       nrows * ncols.\n154 | \n155 |     sharex : bool\n156 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n157 | \n158 |     sharey : bool\n159 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me check the plotting accessor implementation:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor`.\n```\n 620 | class PlotAccessor(PandasObject):\n 621 |     \"\"\"\n 622 |     Make plots of Series or DataFrame.\n 623 | \n 624 |     Uses the backend specified by the\n 625 |     option ``plotting.backend``. By default, matplotlib is used.\n 626 | \n 627 |     Parameters\n 628 |     ----------\n 629 |     data : Series or DataFrame\n 630 |         The object for which the method is called.\n 631 |     x : label or position, default None\n 632 |         Only used if data is a DataFrame.\n 633 |     y : label, position or list of label, positions, default None\n 634 |         Allows plotting of one column versus another. Only used if data is a\n 635 |         DataFrame.\n 636 |     kind : str\n 637 |         The kind of plot to produce:\n 638 | \n 639 |         - 'line' : line plot (default)\n 640 |         - 'bar' : vertical bar plot\n 641 |         - 'barh' : horizontal bar plot\n 642 |         - 'hist' : histogram\n 643 |         - 'box' : boxplot\n 644 |         - 'kde' : Kernel Density Estimation plot\n 645 |         - 'density' : same as 'kde'\n 646 |         - 'area' : area plot\n 647 |         - 'pie' : pie plot\n 648 |         - 'scatter' : scatter plot (DataFrame only)\n 649 |         - 'hexbin' : hexbin plot (DataFrame only)\n 650 |     ax : matplotlib axes object, default None\n 651 |         An axes of the current figure.\n 652 |     subplots : bool, default False\n 653 |         Make separate subplots for each column.\n 654 |     sharex : bool, default True if ax is None else False\n 655 |         In case ``subplots=True``, share x axis and set some x axis labels\n 656 |         to invisible; defaults to True if ax is None otherwise False if\n 657 |         an ax is passed in; Be aware, that passing in both an ax and\n 658 |         ``sharex=True`` will alter all x axis labels for all axis in a figure.\n 659 |     sharey : bool, default False\n 660 |         In case ``subplots=True``, share y axis and set some y axis labels to invisible.\n 661 |     layout : tuple, optional\n 662 |         (rows, columns) for the layout of subplots.\n 663 |     figsize : a tuple (width, height) in inches\n 664 |         Size of a figure object.\n 665 |     use_index : bool, default True\n 666 |         Use index as ticks for x axis.\n 667 |     title : str or list\n 668 |         Title to use for the plot. If a string is passed, print the string\n 669 |         at the top of the figure. If a list is passed and `subplots` is\n 670 |         True, print each item in the list above the corresponding subplot.\n 671 |     grid : bool, default None (matlab style default)\n 672 |         Axis grid lines.\n 673 |     legend : bool or {'reverse'}\n 674 |         Place legend on axis subplots.\n 675 |     style : list or dict\n 676 |         The matplotlib line style per column.\n 677 |     logx : bool or 'sym', default False\n 678 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on x axis.\n 679 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 680 | \n 681 |     logy : bool or 'sym' default False\n 682 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on y axis.\n 683 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 684 | \n 685 |     loglog : bool or 'sym', default False\n 686 |         Use log scaling or symlog scaling on both x and y axes.\n 687 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.25.0\n 688 | \n 689 |     xticks : sequence\n 690 |         Values to use for the xticks.\n 691 |     yticks : sequence\n 692 |         Values to use for the yticks.\n 693 |     xlim : 2-tuple/list\n 694 |         Set the x limits of the current axes.\n 695 |     ylim : 2-tuple/list\n 696 |         Set the y limits of the current axes.\n 697 |     xlabel : label, optional\n 698 |         Name to use for the xlabel on x-axis. Default uses index name as xlabel, or the\n 699 |         x-column name for planar plots.\n 700 | \n 701 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 702 | \n 703 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.2.0\n 704 | \n 705 |            Now applicable to planar plots (`scatter`, `hexbin`).\n 706 | \n 707 |     ylabel : label, optional\n 708 |         Name to use for the ylabel on y-axis. Default will show no ylabel, or the\n 709 |         y-column name for planar plots.\n 710 | \n 711 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 712 | \n 713 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.2.0\n 714 | \n 715 |            Now applicable to planar plots (`scatter`, `hexbin`).\n 716 | \n 717 |     rot : int, default None\n 718 |         Rotation for ticks (xticks for vertical, yticks for horizontal\n 719 |         plots).\n 720 |     fontsize : int, default None\n 721 |         Font size for xticks and yticks.\n 722 |     colormap : str or matplotlib colormap object, default None\n 723 |         Colormap to select colors from. If string, load colormap with that\n 724 |         name from matplotlib.\n 725 |     colorbar : bool, optional\n 726 |         If True, plot colorbar (only relevant for 'scatter' and 'hexbin'\n 727 |         plots).\n 728 |     position : float\n 729 |         Specify relative alignments for bar plot layout.\n 730 |         From 0 (left/bottom-end) to 1 (right/top-end). Default is 0.5\n 731 |         (center).\n 732 |     table : bool, Series or DataFrame, default False\n 733 |         If True, draw a table using the data in the DataFrame and the data\n 734 |         will be transposed to meet matplotlib's default layout.\n 735 |         If a Series or DataFrame is passed, use passed data to draw a\n 736 |         table.\n 737 |     yerr : DataFrame, Series, array-like, dict and str\n 738 |         See :ref:`Plotting with Error Bars <visualization.errorbars>` for\n 739 |         detail.\n 740 |     xerr : DataFrame, Series, array-like, dict and str\n 741 |         Equivalent to yerr.\n 742 |     stacked : bool, default False in line and bar plots, and True in area plot\n 743 |         If True, create stacked plot.\n 744 |     sort_columns : bool, default False\n 745 |         Sort column names to determine plot ordering.\n 746 |     secondary_y : bool or sequence, default False\n 747 |         Whether to plot on the secondary y-axis if a list/tuple, which\n 748 |         columns to plot on secondary y-axis.\n 749 |     mark_right : bool, default True\n 750 |         When using a secondary_y axis, automatically mark the column\n 751 |         labels with \"(right)\" in the legend.\n 752 |     include_bool : bool, default is False\n 753 |         If True, boolean values can be plotted.\n 754 |     backend : str, default None\n 755 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n 756 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n 757 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n 758 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n 759 | \n 760 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n 761 | \n 762 |     **kwargs\n 763 |         Options to pass to matplotlib plotting method.\n 764 | \n 765 |     Returns\n 766 |     -------\n 767 |     :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n 768 |         If the backend is not the default matplotlib one, the return value\n 769 |         will be the object returned by the backend.\n 770 | \n 771 |     Notes\n 772 |     -----\n 773 |     - See matplotlib documentation online for more on this subject\n 774 |     - If `kind` = 'bar' or 'barh', you can specify relative alignments\n 775 |       for bar plot layout by `position` keyword.\n 776 |       From 0 (left/bottom-end) to 1 (right/top-end). Default is 0.5\n 777 |       (center)\n 778 |     \"\"\"\n 779 | \n 780 |     _common_kinds = (\"line\", \"bar\", \"barh\", \"kde\", \"density\", \"area\", \"hist\", \"box\")\n 781 |     _series_kinds = (\"pie\",)\n 782 |     _dataframe_kinds = (\"scatter\", \"hexbin\")\n 783 |     _kind_aliases = {\"density\": \"kde\"}\n 784 |     _all_kinds = _common_kinds + _series_kinds + _dataframe_kinds\n 785 | \n 786 |     def __init__(self, data) -> None:\n 787 |         self._parent = data\n 788 | \n 789 |     @staticmethod\n 790 |     def _get_call_args(backend_name, data, args, kwargs):\n 791 |         \"\"\"\n 792 |         This function makes calls to this accessor `__call__` method compatible\n 793 |         with the previous `SeriesPlotMethods.__call__` and\n 794 |         `DataFramePlotMethods.__call__`. Those had slightly different\n 795 |         signatures, since `DataFramePlotMethods` accepted `x` and `y`\n 796 |         parameters.\n 797 |         \"\"\"\n 798 |         if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 799 |             arg_def = [\n 800 |                 (\"kind\", \"line\"),\n 801 |                 (\"ax\", None),\n 802 |                 (\"figsize\", None),\n 803 |                 (\"use_index\", True),\n 804 |                 (\"title\", None),\n 805 |                 (\"grid\", None),\n 806 |                 (\"legend\", False),\n 807 |                 (\"style\", None),\n 808 |                 (\"logx\", False),\n 809 |                 (\"logy\", False),\n 810 |                 (\"loglog\", False),\n 811 |                 (\"xticks\", None),\n 812 |                 (\"yticks\", None),\n 813 |                 (\"xlim\", None),\n 814 |                 (\"ylim\", None),\n 815 |                 (\"rot\", None),\n 816 |                 (\"fontsize\", None),\n 817 |                 (\"colormap\", None),\n 818 |                 (\"table\", False),\n 819 |                 (\"yerr\", None),\n 820 |                 (\"xerr\", None),\n 821 |                 (\"label\", None),\n 822 |                 (\"secondary_y\", False),\n 823 |                 (\"xlabel\", None),\n 824 |                 (\"ylabel\", None),\n 825 |             ]\n 826 |         elif isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 827 |             arg_def = [\n 828 |                 (\"x\", None),\n 829 |                 (\"y\", None),\n 830 |                 (\"kind\", \"line\"),\n 831 |                 (\"ax\", None),\n 832 |                 (\"subplots\", False),\n 833 |                 (\"sharex\", None),\n 834 |                 (\"sharey\", False),\n 835 |                 (\"layout\", None),\n 836 |                 (\"figsize\", None),\n 837 |                 (\"use_index\", True),\n 838 |                 (\"title\", None),\n 839 |                 (\"grid\", None),\n 840 |                 (\"legend\", True),\n 841 |                 (\"style\", None),\n 842 |                 (\"logx\", False),\n 843 |                 (\"logy\", False),\n 844 |                 (\"loglog\", False),\n 845 |                 (\"xticks\", None),\n 846 |                 (\"yticks\", None),\n 847 |                 (\"xlim\", None),\n 848 |                 (\"ylim\", None),\n 849 |                 (\"rot\", None),\n 850 |                 (\"fontsize\", None),\n 851 |                 (\"colormap\", None),\n 852 |                 (\"table\", False),\n 853 |                 (\"yerr\", None),\n 854 |                 (\"xerr\", None),\n 855 |                 (\"secondary_y\", False),\n 856 |                 (\"sort_columns\", False),\n 857 |                 (\"xlabel\", None),\n 858 |                 (\"ylabel\", None),\n 859 |             ]\n 860 |         else:\n 861 |             raise TypeError(\n 862 |                 f\"Called plot accessor for type {type(data).__name__}, \"\n 863 |                 \"expected Series or DataFrame\"\n 864 |             )\n 865 | \n 866 |         if args and isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 867 |             positional_args = str(args)[1:-1]\n 868 |             keyword_args = \", \".join(\n 869 |                 [f\"{name}={repr(value)}\" for (name, _), value in zip(arg_def, args)]\n 870 |             )\n 871 |             msg = (\n 872 |                 \"`Series.plot()` should not be called with positional \"\n 873 |                 \"arguments, only keyword arguments. The order of \"\n 874 |                 \"positional arguments will change in the future. \"\n 875 |                 f\"Use `Series.plot({keyword_args})` instead of \"\n 876 |                 f\"`Series.plot({positional_args})`.\"\n 877 |             )\n 878 |             raise TypeError(msg)\n 879 | \n 880 |         pos_args = {name: value for (name, _), value in zip(arg_def, args)}\n 881 |         if backend_name == \"pandas.plotting._matplotlib\":\n 882 |             kwargs = dict(arg_def, **pos_args, **kwargs)\n 883 |         else:\n 884 |             kwargs = dict(pos_args, **kwargs)\n 885 | \n 886 |         x = kwargs.pop(\"x\", None)\n 887 |         y = kwargs.pop(\"y\", None)\n 888 |         kind = kwargs.pop(\"kind\", \"line\")\n 889 |         return x, y, kind, kwargs\n 890 | \n 891 |     def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n 892 |         plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(kwargs.pop(\"backend\", None))\n 893 | \n 894 |         x, y, kind, kwargs = self._get_call_args(\n 895 |             plot_backend.__name__, self._parent, args, kwargs\n 896 |         )\n 897 | \n 898 |         kind = self._kind_aliases.get(kind, kind)\n 899 | \n 900 |         # when using another backend, get out of the way\n 901 |         if plot_backend.__name__ != \"pandas.plotting._matplotlib\":\n 902 |             return plot_backend.plot(self._parent, x=x, y=y, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 903 | \n 904 |         if kind not in self._all_kinds:\n 905 |             raise ValueError(f\"{kind} is not a valid plot kind\")\n 906 | \n 907 |         # The original data structured can be transformed before passed to the\n 908 |         # backend. For example, for DataFrame is common to set the index as the\n 909 |         # `x` parameter, and return a Series with the parameter `y` as values.\n 910 |         data = self._parent.copy()\n 911 | \n 912 |         if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 913 |             kwargs[\"reuse_plot\"] = True\n 914 | \n 915 |         if kind in self._dataframe_kinds:\n 916 |             if isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 917 |                 return plot_backend.plot(data, x=x, y=y, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 918 |             else:\n 919 |                 raise ValueError(f\"plot kind {kind} can only be used for data frames\")\n 920 |         elif kind in self._series_kinds:\n 921 |             if isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 922 |                 if y is None and kwargs.get(\"subplots\") is False:\n 923 |                     raise ValueError(\n 924 |                         f\"{kind} requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\"\n 925 |                     )\n 926 |                 elif y is not None:\n 927 |                     if is_integer(y) and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 928 |                         y = data.columns[y]\n 929 |                     # converted to series actually. copy to not modify\n 930 |                     data = data[y].copy()\n 931 |                     data.index.name = y\n 932 |         elif isinstance(data, ABCDataFrame):\n 933 |             data_cols = data.columns\n 934 |             if x is not None:\n 935 |                 if is_integer(x) and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 936 |                     x = data_cols[x]\n 937 |                 elif not isinstance(data[x], ABCSeries):\n 938 |                     raise ValueError(\"x must be a label or position\")\n 939 |                 data = data.set_index(x)\n 940 |             if y is not None:\n 941 |                 # check if we have y as int or list of ints\n 942 |                 int_ylist = is_list_like(y) and all(is_integer(c) for c in y)\n 943 |                 int_y_arg = is_integer(y) or int_ylist\n 944 |                 if int_y_arg and not data.columns.holds_integer():\n 945 |                     y = data_cols[y]\n 946 | \n 947 |                 label_kw = kwargs[\"label\"] if \"label\" in kwargs else False\n 948 |                 for kw in [\"xerr\", \"yerr\"]:\n 949 |                     if kw in kwargs and (\n 950 |                         isinstance(kwargs[kw], str) or is_integer(kwargs[kw])\n 951 |                     ):\n 952 |                         try:\n 953 |                             kwargs[kw] = data[kwargs[kw]]\n 954 |                         except (IndexError, KeyError, TypeError):\n 955 |                             pass\n 956 | \n 957 |                 # don't overwrite\n 958 |                 data = data[y].copy()\n 959 | \n 960 |                 if isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n 961 |                     label_name = label_kw or y\n 962 |                     data.name = label_name\n 963 |                 else:\n 964 |                     match = is_list_like(label_kw) and len(label_kw) == len(y)\n 965 |                     if label_kw and not match:\n 966 |                         raise ValueError(\n 967 |                             \"label should be list-like and same length as y\"\n 968 |                         )\n 969 |                     label_name = label_kw or data.columns\n 970 |                     data.columns = label_name\n 971 | \n 972 |         return plot_backend.plot(data, kind=kind, **kwargs)\n 973 | \n 974 |     __call__.__doc__ = __doc__\n 975 | \n 976 |     @Appender(\n 977 |         \"\"\"\n 978 |         See Also\n 979 |         --------\n 980 |         matplotlib.pyplot.plot : Plot y versus x as lines and/or markers.\n 981 | \n 982 |         Examples\n 983 |         --------\n 984 | \n 985 |         .. plot::\n 986 |             :context: close-figs\n 987 | \n 988 |             >>> s = pd.Series([1, 3, 2])\n 989 |             >>> s.plot.line()\n 990 |             <AxesSubplot:ylabel='Density'>\n 991 | \n 992 |         .. plot::\n 993 |             :context: close-figs\n 994 | \n 995 |             The following example shows the populations for some animals\n 996 |             over the years.\n 997 | \n 998 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n 999 |             ...    'pig': [20, 18, 489, 675, 1776],\n1000 |             ...    'horse': [4, 25, 281, 600, 1900]\n1001 |             ...    }, index=[1990, 1997, 2003, 2009, 2014])\n1002 |             >>> lines = df.plot.line()\n1003 | \n1004 |         .. plot::\n1005 |            :context: close-figs\n1006 | \n1007 |            An example with subplots, so an array of axes is returned.\n1008 | \n1009 |            >>> axes = df.plot.line(subplots=True)\n1010 |            >>> type(axes)\n1011 |            <class 'numpy.ndarray'>\n1012 | \n1013 |         .. plot::\n1014 |            :context: close-figs\n1015 | \n1016 |            Let's repeat the same example, but specifying colors for\n1017 |            each column (in this case, for each animal).\n1018 | \n1019 |            >>> axes = df.plot.line(\n1020 |            ...     subplots=True, color={\"pig\": \"pink\", \"horse\": \"#742802\"}\n1021 |            ... )\n1022 | \n1023 |         .. plot::\n1024 |             :context: close-figs\n1025 | \n1026 |             The following example shows the relationship between both\n1027 |             populations.\n1028 | \n1029 |             >>> lines = df.plot.line(x='pig', y='horse')\n1030 |         \"\"\"\n1031 |     )\n1032 |     @Substitution(kind=\"line\")\n1033 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1034 |     def line(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1035 |         \"\"\"\n1036 |         Plot Series or DataFrame as lines.\n1037 | \n1038 |         This function is useful to plot lines using DataFrame's values\n1039 |         as coordinates.\n1040 |         \"\"\"\n1041 |         return self(kind=\"line\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1042 | \n1043 |     @Appender(\n1044 |         \"\"\"\n1045 |         See Also\n1046 |         --------\n1047 |         DataFrame.plot.barh : Horizontal bar plot.\n1048 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1049 |         matplotlib.pyplot.bar : Make a bar plot with matplotlib.\n1050 | \n1051 |         Examples\n1052 |         --------\n1053 |         Basic plot.\n1054 | \n1055 |         .. plot::\n1056 |             :context: close-figs\n1057 | \n1058 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab':['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val':[10, 30, 20]})\n1059 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lab', y='val', rot=0)\n1060 | \n1061 |         Plot a whole dataframe to a bar plot. Each column is assigned a\n1062 |         distinct color, and each row is nested in a group along the\n1063 |         horizontal axis.\n1064 | \n1065 |         .. plot::\n1066 |             :context: close-figs\n1067 | \n1068 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1069 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1070 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1071 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1072 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1073 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1074 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(rot=0)\n1075 | \n1076 |         Plot stacked bar charts for the DataFrame\n1077 | \n1078 |         .. plot::\n1079 |             :context: close-figs\n1080 | \n1081 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(stacked=True)\n1082 | \n1083 |         Instead of nesting, the figure can be split by column with\n1084 |         ``subplots=True``. In this case, a :class:`numpy.ndarray` of\n1085 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` are returned.\n1086 | \n1087 |         .. plot::\n1088 |             :context: close-figs\n1089 | \n1090 |             >>> axes = df.plot.bar(rot=0, subplots=True)\n1091 |             >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1092 | \n1093 |         If you don't like the default colours, you can specify how you'd\n1094 |         like each column to be colored.\n1095 | \n1096 |         .. plot::\n1097 |             :context: close-figs\n1098 | \n1099 |             >>> axes = df.plot.bar(\n1100 |             ...     rot=0, subplots=True, color={\"speed\": \"red\", \"lifespan\": \"green\"}\n1101 |             ... )\n1102 |             >>> axes[1].legend(loc=2)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1103 | \n1104 |         Plot a single column.\n1105 | \n1106 |         .. plot::\n1107 |             :context: close-figs\n1108 | \n1109 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(y='speed', rot=0)\n1110 | \n1111 |         Plot only selected categories for the DataFrame.\n1112 | \n1113 |         .. plot::\n1114 |             :context: close-figs\n1115 | \n1116 |             >>> ax = df.plot.bar(x='lifespan', rot=0)\n1117 |     \"\"\"\n1118 |     )\n1119 |     @Substitution(kind=\"bar\")\n1120 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1121 |     def bar(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1122 |         \"\"\"\n1123 |         Vertical bar plot.\n1124 | \n1125 |         A bar plot is a plot that presents categorical data with\n1126 |         rectangular bars with lengths proportional to the values that they\n1127 |         represent. A bar plot shows comparisons among discrete categories. One\n1128 |         axis of the plot shows the specific categories being compared, and the\n1129 |         other axis represents a measured value.\n1130 |         \"\"\"\n1131 |         return self(kind=\"bar\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1132 | \n1133 |     @Appender(\n1134 |         \"\"\"\n1135 |         See Also\n1136 |         --------\n1137 |         DataFrame.plot.bar: Vertical bar plot.\n1138 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib.\n1139 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar : Plot a vertical bar plot using matplotlib.\n1140 | \n1141 |         Examples\n1142 |         --------\n1143 |         Basic example\n1144 | \n1145 |         .. plot::\n1146 |             :context: close-figs\n1147 | \n1148 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'lab': ['A', 'B', 'C'], 'val': [10, 30, 20]})\n1149 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(x='lab', y='val')\n1150 | \n1151 |         Plot a whole DataFrame to a horizontal bar plot\n1152 | \n1153 |         .. plot::\n1154 |             :context: close-figs\n1155 | \n1156 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1157 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1158 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1159 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1160 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1161 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1162 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh()\n1163 | \n1164 |         Plot stacked barh charts for the DataFrame\n1165 | \n1166 |         .. plot::\n1167 |             :context: close-figs\n1168 | \n1169 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(stacked=True)\n1170 | \n1171 |         We can specify colors for each column\n1172 | \n1173 |         .. plot::\n1174 |             :context: close-figs\n1175 | \n1176 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(color={\"speed\": \"red\", \"lifespan\": \"green\"})\n1177 | \n1178 |         Plot a column of the DataFrame to a horizontal bar plot\n1179 | \n1180 |         .. plot::\n1181 |             :context: close-figs\n1182 | \n1183 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1184 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1185 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1186 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1187 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1188 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1189 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(y='speed')\n1190 | \n1191 |         Plot DataFrame versus the desired column\n1192 | \n1193 |         .. plot::\n1194 |             :context: close-figs\n1195 | \n1196 |             >>> speed = [0.1, 17.5, 40, 48, 52, 69, 88]\n1197 |             >>> lifespan = [2, 8, 70, 1.5, 25, 12, 28]\n1198 |             >>> index = ['snail', 'pig', 'elephant',\n1199 |             ...          'rabbit', 'giraffe', 'coyote', 'horse']\n1200 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'speed': speed,\n1201 |             ...                    'lifespan': lifespan}, index=index)\n1202 |             >>> ax = df.plot.barh(x='lifespan')\n1203 |     \"\"\"\n1204 |     )\n1205 |     @Substitution(kind=\"bar\")\n1206 |     @Appender(_bar_or_line_doc)\n1207 |     def barh(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1208 |         \"\"\"\n1209 |         Make a horizontal bar plot.\n1210 | \n1211 |         A horizontal bar plot is a plot that presents quantitative data with\n1212 |         rectangular bars with lengths proportional to the values that they\n1213 |         represent. A bar plot shows comparisons among discrete categories. One\n1214 |         axis of the plot shows the specific categories being compared, and the\n1215 |         other axis represents a measured value.\n1216 |         \"\"\"\n1217 |         return self(kind=\"barh\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1218 | \n1219 |     def box(self, by=None, **kwargs):\n1220 |         r\"\"\"\n1221 |         Make a box plot of the DataFrame columns.\n1222 | \n1223 |         A box plot is a method for graphically depicting groups of numerical\n1224 |         data through their quartiles.\n1225 |         The box extends from the Q1 to Q3 quartile values of the data,\n1226 |         with a line at the median (Q2). The whiskers extend from the edges\n1227 |         of box to show the range of the data. The position of the whiskers\n1228 |         is set by default to 1.5*IQR (IQR = Q3 - Q1) from the edges of the\n1229 |         box. Outlier points are those past the end of the whiskers.\n1230 | \n1231 |         For further details see Wikipedia's\n1232 |         entry for `boxplot <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box_plot>`__.\n1233 | \n1234 |         A consideration when using this chart is that the box and the whiskers\n1235 |         can overlap, which is very common when plotting small sets of data.\n1236 | \n1237 |         Parameters\n1238 |         ----------\n1239 |         by : str or sequence\n1240 |             Column in the DataFrame to group by.\n1241 | \n1242 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1243 | \n1244 |                Previously, `by` is silently ignore and makes no groupings\n1245 | \n1246 |         **kwargs\n1247 |             Additional keywords are documented in\n1248 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1249 | \n1250 |         Returns\n1251 |         -------\n1252 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1253 | \n1254 |         See Also\n1255 |         --------\n1256 |         DataFrame.boxplot: Another method to draw a box plot.\n1257 |         Series.plot.box: Draw a box plot from a Series object.\n1258 |         matplotlib.pyplot.boxplot: Draw a box plot in matplotlib.\n1259 | \n1260 |         Examples\n1261 |         --------\n1262 |         Draw a box plot from a DataFrame with four columns of randomly\n1263 |         generated data.\n1264 | \n1265 |         .. plot::\n1266 |             :context: close-figs\n1267 | \n1268 |             >>> data = np.random.randn(25, 4)\n1269 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'))\n1270 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box()\n1271 | \n1272 |         You can also generate groupings if you specify the `by` parameter (which\n1273 |         can take a column name, or a list or tuple of column names):\n1274 | \n1275 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1276 | \n1277 |         .. plot::\n1278 |             :context: close-figs\n1279 | \n1280 |             >>> age_list = [8, 10, 12, 14, 72, 74, 76, 78, 20, 25, 30, 35, 60, 85]\n1281 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"gender\": list(\"MMMMMMMMFFFFFF\"), \"age\": age_list})\n1282 |             >>> ax = df.plot.box(column=\"age\", by=\"gender\", figsize=(10, 8))\n1283 |         \"\"\"\n1284 |         return self(kind=\"box\", by=by, **kwargs)\n1285 | \n1286 |     def hist(self, by=None, bins=10, **kwargs):\n1287 |         \"\"\"\n1288 |         Draw one histogram of the DataFrame's columns.\n1289 | \n1290 |         A histogram is a representation of the distribution of data.\n1291 |         This function groups the values of all given Series in the DataFrame\n1292 |         into bins and draws all bins in one :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes`.\n1293 |         This is useful when the DataFrame's Series are in a similar scale.\n1294 | \n1295 |         Parameters\n1296 |         ----------\n1297 |         by : str or sequence, optional\n1298 |             Column in the DataFrame to group by.\n1299 | \n1300 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1301 | \n1302 |                Previously, `by` is silently ignore and makes no groupings\n1303 | \n1304 |         bins : int, default 10\n1305 |             Number of histogram bins to be used.\n1306 |         **kwargs\n1307 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1308 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1309 | \n1310 |         Returns\n1311 |         -------\n1312 |         class:`matplotlib.AxesSubplot`\n1313 |             Return a histogram plot.\n1314 | \n1315 |         See Also\n1316 |         --------\n1317 |         DataFrame.hist : Draw histograms per DataFrame's Series.\n1318 |         Series.hist : Draw a histogram with Series' data.\n1319 | \n1320 |         Examples\n1321 |         --------\n1322 |         When we roll a die 6000 times, we expect to get each value around 1000\n1323 |         times. But when we roll two dice and sum the result, the distribution\n1324 |         is going to be quite different. A histogram illustrates those\n1325 |         distributions.\n1326 | \n1327 |         .. plot::\n1328 |             :context: close-figs\n1329 | \n1330 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1331 |             ...     np.random.randint(1, 7, 6000),\n1332 |             ...     columns = ['one'])\n1333 |             >>> df['two'] = df['one'] + np.random.randint(1, 7, 6000)\n1334 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hist(bins=12, alpha=0.5)\n1335 | \n1336 |         A grouped histogram can be generated by providing the parameter `by` (which\n1337 |         can be a column name, or a list of column names):\n1338 | \n1339 |         .. plot::\n1340 |             :context: close-figs\n1341 | \n1342 |             >>> age_list = [8, 10, 12, 14, 72, 74, 76, 78, 20, 25, 30, 35, 60, 85]\n1343 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"gender\": list(\"MMMMMMMMFFFFFF\"), \"age\": age_list})\n1344 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hist(column=[\"age\"], by=\"gender\", figsize=(10, 8))\n1345 |         \"\"\"\n1346 |         return self(kind=\"hist\", by=by, bins=bins, **kwargs)\n1347 | \n1348 |     def kde(self, bw_method=None, ind=None, **kwargs):\n1349 |         \"\"\"\n1350 |         Generate Kernel Density Estimate plot using Gaussian kernels.\n1351 | \n1352 |         In statistics, `kernel density estimation`_ (KDE) is a non-parametric\n1353 |         way to estimate the probability density function (PDF) of a random\n1354 |         variable. This function uses Gaussian kernels and includes automatic\n1355 |         bandwidth determination.\n1356 | \n1357 |         .. _kernel density estimation:\n1358 |             https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_density_estimation\n1359 | \n1360 |         Parameters\n1361 |         ----------\n1362 |         bw_method : str, scalar or callable, optional\n1363 |             The method used to calculate the estimator bandwidth. This can be\n1364 |             'scott', 'silverman', a scalar constant or a callable.\n1365 |             If None (default), 'scott' is used.\n1366 |             See :class:`scipy.stats.gaussian_kde` for more information.\n1367 |         ind : NumPy array or int, optional\n1368 |             Evaluation points for the estimated PDF. If None (default),\n1369 |             1000 equally spaced points are used. If `ind` is a NumPy array, the\n1370 |             KDE is evaluated at the points passed. If `ind` is an integer,\n1371 |             `ind` number of equally spaced points are used.\n1372 |         **kwargs\n1373 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1374 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1375 | \n1376 |         Returns\n1377 |         -------\n1378 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or numpy.ndarray of them\n1379 | \n1380 |         See Also\n1381 |         --------\n1382 |         scipy.stats.gaussian_kde : Representation of a kernel-density\n1383 |             estimate using Gaussian kernels. This is the function used\n1384 |             internally to estimate the PDF.\n1385 | \n1386 |         Examples\n1387 |         --------\n1388 |         Given a Series of points randomly sampled from an unknown\n1389 |         distribution, estimate its PDF using KDE with automatic\n1390 |         bandwidth determination and plot the results, evaluating them at\n1391 |         1000 equally spaced points (default):\n1392 | \n1393 |         .. plot::\n1394 |             :context: close-figs\n1395 | \n1396 |             >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 5])\n1397 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde()\n1398 | \n1399 |         A scalar bandwidth can be specified. Using a small bandwidth value can\n1400 |         lead to over-fitting, while using a large bandwidth value may result\n1401 |         in under-fitting:\n1402 | \n1403 |         .. plot::\n1404 |             :context: close-figs\n1405 | \n1406 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(bw_method=0.3)\n1407 | \n1408 |         .. plot::\n1409 |             :context: close-figs\n1410 | \n1411 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(bw_method=3)\n1412 | \n1413 |         Finally, the `ind` parameter determines the evaluation points for the\n1414 |         plot of the estimated PDF:\n1415 | \n1416 |         .. plot::\n1417 |             :context: close-figs\n1418 | \n1419 |             >>> ax = s.plot.kde(ind=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n1420 | \n1421 |         For DataFrame, it works in the same way:\n1422 | \n1423 |         .. plot::\n1424 |             :context: close-figs\n1425 | \n1426 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1427 |             ...     'x': [1, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5, 4, 5],\n1428 |             ...     'y': [4, 4, 4.5, 5, 5.5, 6, 6],\n1429 |             ... })\n1430 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde()\n1431 | \n1432 |         A scalar bandwidth can be specified. Using a small bandwidth value can\n1433 |         lead to over-fitting, while using a large bandwidth value may result\n1434 |         in under-fitting:\n1435 | \n1436 |         .. plot::\n1437 |             :context: close-figs\n1438 | \n1439 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(bw_method=0.3)\n1440 | \n1441 |         .. plot::\n1442 |             :context: close-figs\n1443 | \n1444 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(bw_method=3)\n1445 | \n1446 |         Finally, the `ind` parameter determines the evaluation points for the\n1447 |         plot of the estimated PDF:\n1448 | \n1449 |         .. plot::\n1450 |             :context: close-figs\n1451 | \n1452 |             >>> ax = df.plot.kde(ind=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\n1453 |         \"\"\"\n1454 |         return self(kind=\"kde\", bw_method=bw_method, ind=ind, **kwargs)\n1455 | \n1456 |     density = kde\n1457 | \n1458 |     def area(self, x=None, y=None, **kwargs):\n1459 |         \"\"\"\n1460 |         Draw a stacked area plot.\n1461 | \n1462 |         An area plot displays quantitative data visually.\n1463 |         This function wraps the matplotlib area function.\n1464 | \n1465 |         Parameters\n1466 |         ----------\n1467 |         x : label or position, optional\n1468 |             Coordinates for the X axis. By default uses the index.\n1469 |         y : label or position, optional\n1470 |             Column to plot. By default uses all columns.\n1471 |         stacked : bool, default True\n1472 |             Area plots are stacked by default. Set to False to create a\n1473 |             unstacked plot.\n1474 |         **kwargs\n1475 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1476 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1477 | \n1478 |         Returns\n1479 |         -------\n1480 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or numpy.ndarray\n1481 |             Area plot, or array of area plots if subplots is True.\n1482 | \n1483 |         See Also\n1484 |         --------\n1485 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib / pylab.\n1486 | \n1487 |         Examples\n1488 |         --------\n1489 |         Draw an area plot based on basic business metrics:\n1490 | \n1491 |         .. plot::\n1492 |             :context: close-figs\n1493 | \n1494 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1495 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3, 9, 10, 6],\n1496 |             ...     'signups': [5, 5, 6, 12, 14, 13],\n1497 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28, 62, 81, 50],\n1498 |             ... }, index=pd.date_range(start='2018/01/01', end='2018/07/01',\n1499 |             ...                        freq='M'))\n1500 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area()\n1501 | \n1502 |         Area plots are stacked by default. To produce an unstacked plot,\n1503 |         pass ``stacked=False``:\n1504 | \n1505 |         .. plot::\n1506 |             :context: close-figs\n1507 | \n1508 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(stacked=False)\n1509 | \n1510 |         Draw an area plot for a single column:\n1511 | \n1512 |         .. plot::\n1513 |             :context: close-figs\n1514 | \n1515 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(y='sales')\n1516 | \n1517 |         Draw with a different `x`:\n1518 | \n1519 |         .. plot::\n1520 |             :context: close-figs\n1521 | \n1522 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1523 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3],\n1524 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28],\n1525 |             ...     'day': [1, 2, 3],\n1526 |             ... })\n1527 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(x='day')\n1528 |         \"\"\"\n1529 |         return self(kind=\"area\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1530 | \n1531 |     def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n1585 | \n1586 |     def scatter(self, x, y, s=None, c=None, **kwargs):\n1587 |         \"\"\"\n1588 |         Create a scatter plot with varying marker point size and color.\n1589 | \n1590 |         The coordinates of each point are defined by two dataframe columns and\n1591 |         filled circles are used to represent each point. This kind of plot is\n1592 |         useful to see complex correlations between two variables. Points could\n1593 |         be for instance natural 2D coordinates like longitude and latitude in\n1594 |         a map or, in general, any pair of metrics that can be plotted against\n1595 |         each other.\n1596 | \n1597 |         Parameters\n1598 |         ----------\n1599 |         x : int or str\n1600 |             The column name or column position to be used as horizontal\n1601 |             coordinates for each point.\n1602 |         y : int or str\n1603 |             The column name or column position to be used as vertical\n1604 |             coordinates for each point.\n1605 |         s : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1606 |             The size of each point. Possible values are:\n1607 | \n1608 |             - A string with the name of the column to be used for marker's size.\n1609 | \n1610 |             - A single scalar so all points have the same size.\n1611 | \n1612 |             - A sequence of scalars, which will be used for each point's size\n1613 |               recursively. For instance, when passing [2,14] all points size\n1614 |               will be either 2 or 14, alternatively.\n1615 | \n1616 |               .. versionchanged:: 1.1.0\n1617 | \n1618 |         size : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1619 |             Alias for s.\n1620 | \n1621 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1622 | \n1623 |         c : str, int or array-like, optional\n1624 |             The color of each point. Possible values are:\n1625 | \n1626 |             - A single color string referred to by name, RGB or RGBA code,\n1627 |               for instance 'red' or '#a98d19'.\n1628 | \n1629 |             - A sequence of color strings referred to by name, RGB or RGBA\n1630 |               code, which will be used for each point's color recursively. For\n1631 |               instance ['green','yellow'] all points will be filled in green or\n1632 |               yellow, alternatively.\n1633 | \n1634 |             - A column name or position whose values will be used to color the\n1635 |               marker points according to a colormap.\n1636 |         color : str, int or array-like, optional\n1637 |             Alias for c.\n1638 | \n1639 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1640 | \n1641 |         **kwargs\n1642 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1643 | \n1644 |         Returns\n1645 |         -------\n1646 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1647 | \n1648 |         See Also\n1649 |         --------\n1650 |         matplotlib.pyplot.scatter : Scatter plot using multiple input data\n1651 |             formats.\n1652 | \n1653 |         Examples\n1654 |         --------\n1655 |         Let's see how to draw a scatter plot using coordinates from the values\n1656 |         in a DataFrame's columns.\n1657 | \n1658 |         .. plot::\n1659 |             :context: close-figs\n1660 | \n1661 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[5.1, 3.5, 0], [4.9, 3.0, 0], [7.0, 3.2, 1],\n1662 |             ...                    [6.4, 3.2, 1], [5.9, 3.0, 2]],\n1663 |             ...                   columns=['length', 'width', 'species'])\n1664 |             >>> ax1 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1665 |             ...                       y='width',\n1666 |             ...                       c='DarkBlue')\n1667 | \n1668 |         And now with the color determined by a column as well.\n1669 | \n1670 |         .. plot::\n1671 |             :context: close-figs\n1672 | \n1673 |             >>> ax2 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1674 |             ...                       y='width',\n1675 |             ...                       c='species',\n1676 |             ...                       colormap='viridis')\n1677 |         \"\"\"\n1678 |         size = kwargs.pop(\"size\", None)\n1679 |         if s is not None and size is not None:\n1680 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `s` and `size`\")\n1681 |         elif s is not None or size is not None:\n1682 |             kwargs[\"s\"] = s if s is not None else size\n1683 | \n1684 |         color = kwargs.pop(\"color\", None)\n1685 |         if c is not None and color is not None:\n1686 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `c` and `color`\")\n1687 |         elif c is not None or color is not None:\n1688 |             kwargs[\"c\"] = c if c is not None else color\n1689 | \n1690 |         return self(kind=\"scatter\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1691 | \n1692 |     def hexbin(self, x, y, C=None, reduce_C_function=None, gridsize=None, **kwargs):\n1693 |         \"\"\"\n1694 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot.\n1695 | \n1696 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot of `x` versus `y`. If `C` is `None`\n1697 |         (the default), this is a histogram of the number of occurrences\n1698 |         of the observations at ``(x[i], y[i])``.\n1699 | \n1700 |         If `C` is specified, specifies values at given coordinates\n1701 |         ``(x[i], y[i])``. These values are accumulated for each hexagonal\n1702 |         bin and then reduced according to `reduce_C_function`,\n1703 |         having as default the NumPy's mean function (:meth:`numpy.mean`).\n1704 |         (If `C` is specified, it must also be a 1-D sequence\n1705 |         of the same length as `x` and `y`, or a column label.)\n1706 | \n1707 |         Parameters\n1708 |         ----------\n1709 |         x : int or str\n1710 |             The column label or position for x points.\n1711 |         y : int or str\n1712 |             The column label or position for y points.\n1713 |         C : int or str, optional\n1714 |             The column label or position for the value of `(x, y)` point.\n1715 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default `np.mean`\n1716 |             Function of one argument that reduces all the values in a bin to\n1717 |             a single number (e.g. `np.mean`, `np.max`, `np.sum`, `np.std`).\n1718 |         gridsize : int or tuple of (int, int), default 100\n1719 |             The number of hexagons in the x-direction.\n1720 |             The corresponding number of hexagons in the y-direction is\n1721 |             chosen in a way that the hexagons are approximately regular.\n1722 |             Alternatively, gridsize can be a tuple with two elements\n1723 |             specifying the number of hexagons in the x-direction and the\n1724 |             y-direction.\n1725 |         **kwargs\n1726 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1727 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1728 | \n1729 |         Returns\n1730 |         -------\n1731 |         matplotlib.AxesSubplot\n1732 |             The matplotlib ``Axes`` on which the hexbin is plotted.\n1733 | \n1734 |         See Also\n1735 |         --------\n1736 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1737 |         matplotlib.pyplot.hexbin : Hexagonal binning plot using matplotlib,\n1738 |             the matplotlib function that is used under the hood.\n1739 | \n1740 |         Examples\n1741 |         --------\n1742 |         The following examples are generated with random data from\n1743 |         a normal distribution.\n1744 | \n1745 |         .. plot::\n1746 |             :context: close-figs\n1747 | \n1748 |             >>> n = 10000\n1749 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'x': np.random.randn(n),\n1750 |             ...                    'y': np.random.randn(n)})\n1751 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='x', y='y', gridsize=20)\n1752 | \n1753 |         The next example uses `C` and `np.sum` as `reduce_C_function`.\n1754 |         Note that `'observations'` values ranges from 1 to 5 but the result\n1755 |         plot shows values up to more than 25. This is because of the\n1756 |         `reduce_C_function`.\n1757 | \n1758 |         .. plot::\n1759 |             :context: close-figs\n1760 | \n1761 |             >>> n = 500\n1762 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1763 |             ...     'coord_x': np.random.uniform(-3, 3, size=n),\n1764 |             ...     'coord_y': np.random.uniform(30, 50, size=n),\n1765 |             ...     'observations': np.random.randint(1,5, size=n)\n1766 |             ...     })\n1767 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='coord_x',\n1768 |             ...                     y='coord_y',\n1769 |             ...                     C='observations',\n1770 |             ...                     reduce_C_function=np.sum,\n1771 |             ...                     gridsize=10,\n1772 |             ...                     cmap=\"viridis\")\n1773 |         \"\"\"\n1774 |         if reduce_C_function is not None:\n1775 |             kwargs[\"reduce_C_function\"] = reduce_C_function\n1776 |         if gridsize is not None:\n1777 |             kwargs[\"gridsize\"] = gridsize\n1778 | \n1779 |         return self(kind=\"hexbin\", x=x, y=y, C=C, **kwargs)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the trace artifacts to understand the current implementation:\n\n<function=get_trace_artifacts>\n<parameter=artifact_type>all</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_trace_artifacts", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n{\n  \"instance_id\": \"pandas-dev__pandas-29944\",\n  \"trace_summary\": {\n    \"unique_function_count\": 60,\n    \"returned_count\": 8,\n    \"filtered_count\": 60\n  },\n  \"applied_filters\": {\n    \"artifact_type\": \"all\",\n    \"path_contains\": null,\n    \"name_contains\": null,\n    \"start\": 0,\n    \"limit\": 8\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"amin\",\n      \"path\": \"data point 51/DaLIC/<__array_function__ internals>\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": null,\n      \"end_line\": null,\n      \"event_count\": 16,\n      \"call_count\": 2,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 12\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"in1d\",\n      \"path\": \"data point 51/DaLIC/<__array_function__ internals>\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": null,\n      \"end_line\": null,\n      \"event_count\": 8,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 10\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"ones_like\",\n      \"path\": \"data point 51/DaLIC/<__array_function__ internals>\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": null,\n      \"end_line\": null,\n      \"event_count\": 8,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 12\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"setdiff1d\",\n      \"path\": \"data point 51/DaLIC/<__array_function__ internals>\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": null,\n      \"end_line\": null,\n      \"event_count\": 8,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 8\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"safe_sort\",\n      \"path\": \"pandas/core/algorithms.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": 1641,\n      \"end_line\": 1768,\n      \"event_count\": 14,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 9\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"IndexOpsMixin.nunique\",\n      \"path\": \"pandas/core/base.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"method\",\n      \"defined_line\": 995,\n      \"end_line\": 1032,\n      \"event_count\": 6,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 6\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"is_bool_indexer\",\n      \"path\": \"pandas/core/common.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": 96,\n      \"end_line\": 148,\n      \"event_count\": 6,\n      \"call_count\": 1,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 9\n    },\n    {\n      \"qualified_name\": \"is_interval_dtype\",\n      \"path\": \"pandas/core/dtypes/common.py\",\n      \"definition_type\": \"function\",\n      \"defined_line\": 459,\n      \"end_line\": 494,\n      \"event_count\": 15,\n      \"call_count\": 3,\n      \"max_call_depth\": 11\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_more\": true,\n  \"next_start\": 8\n}"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis of the issue and the codebase, I can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the required format:\n\n```\npandas/plotting/_core.py\nline: 652-653\nline: 832-833\nclass: PlotAccessor\nfunction: __init__\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py\nline: 132-312\nfunction: create_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nline: 961-966\nclass: MPLPlot\nfunction: _get_subplots\n\npandas/core/frame.py\nline: 3468-3469\nfunction: T\n```\n\nThese locations are ordered by importance for implementing the feature request. The modifications would primarily focus on the plotting functionality, particularly in the `PlotAccessor` class where the `subplots` parameter is currently handled. The `create_subplots` function in `_matplotlib/tools.py` is responsible for creating the actual subplots, and the `_get_subplots` function in `_matplotlib/core.py` handles getting the existing subplots. The `T` function in `frame.py` is relevant as it's used for transposing data, which might be useful for implementing column grouping.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 295456, "completion_tokens": 614}, "time": 290.48880982398987}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py", "pandas/core/arrays/string_.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/base.py", "pandas/core/construction.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/core/arraylike.py:OpsMixin", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:Fillna", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:GetItemSingleColumn", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/converter.py:MilliSecondLocator", "pandas/core/indexes/range.py:RangeIndex", "pandas/core/indexes/category.py:CategoricalIndex", "pandas/io/html.py:_HtmlFrameParser._parse_thead_tbody_tfoot", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin", "asv_bench/benchmarks/arithmetic.py:ApplyIndex", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Fillna", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/misc.py:autocorrelation_plot", "pandas/core/dtypes/base.py:StorageExtensionDtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:IndexSingleRow", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:AssignTimeseriesIndex", "pandas/io/sas/sas7bdat.py:SAS7BDATReader", "pandas/core/construction.py:extract_array"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/arraylike.py:OpsMixin.__pow__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/groupby.py:Fillna.time_df_ffill", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:GetItemSingleColumn.time_frame_getitem_single_column_label", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/converter.py:MilliSecondLocator._get_interval", "pandas/core/indexes/range.py:RangeIndex.inferred_type", "pandas/core/indexes/category.py:CategoricalIndex._is_comparable_dtype", "pandas/io/html.py:_HtmlFrameParser._parse_thead_tbody_tfoot.row_is_all_th", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_upper", "asv_bench/benchmarks/arithmetic.py:ApplyIndex.time_apply_index", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_isalnum", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Fillna.time_ffill", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/misc.py:autocorrelation_plot.r", "pandas/core/dtypes/base.py:StorageExtensionDtype.__repr__", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_isdigit", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:IndexSingleRow.time_loc_row", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_islower", "pandas/core/dtypes/base.py:StorageExtensionDtype.__hash__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/indexing.py:AssignTimeseriesIndex.time_frame_assign_timeseries_index", "pandas/io/sas/sas7bdat.py:SAS7BDATReader._process_subheader_counts", "pandas/core/strings/object_array.py:ObjectStringArrayMixin._str_isupper", "pandas/core/construction.py:extract_array"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the analysis, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 258\nclass: StringMethods\nfunction: _validate\n\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 245-246\nclass: StringMethods\nfunction: _validate\n\npandas/core/arrays/string_.py\nline: 1\nclass: StringDtype\nfunction: __init__\n\npandas/core/dtypes/base.py\nline: 1\nclass: StorageExtensionDtype\nfunction: __init__\n\npandas/core/construction.py\nline: 425-487\nfunction: extract_array\n```\n\nThe main location that needs modification is in the `StringMethods._validate` function in `accessor.py`, where the error message currently doesn't include the inferred dtype. This is confirmed by the execution trace analysis which shows the error being raised during string accessor validation.\n\nThe other files are listed because they're involved in the string accessor system and might need updates if the error message change affects how these components handle the error:\n\n1. `StringMethods._validate` - This is the main function that needs to be modified to include the inferred dtype in the error message\n2. `StringDtype` - This defines the string dtype representation that might be useful for displaying type information\n3. `StorageExtensionDtype` - Parent class that handles extension dtypes\n4. `extract_array` - Helper function that's used during the validation process\n\nThese locations form the complete picture of where changes would need to be made to implement the requested feature of increasing verbosity on error messages by adding the inferred dtype information."], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 73213, "completion_tokens": 525}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #5: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #5 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - If execution-trace or data-dependency tools are available, use them as compact hints to complement your understanding of the execution flow.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: ENH: increase verbosity on error\n\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental evidence from software dynamic analysis is available through a tool for this instance: `get_trace_ar