5 |         Round each value in a Series to the given number of decimals.\n2466 | \n2467 |         Parameters\n2468 |         ----------\n2469 |         decimals : int, default 0\n2470 |             Number of decimal places to round to. If decimals is negative,\n2471 |             it specifies the number of positions to the left of the decimal point.\n2472 |         *args, **kwargs\n2473 |             Additional arguments and keywords have no effect but might be\n2474 |             accepted for compatibility with NumPy.\n2475 | \n2476 |         Returns\n2477 |         -------\n2478 |         Series\n2479 |             Rounded values of the Series.\n2480 | \n2481 |         See Also\n2482 |         --------\n2483 |         numpy.around : Round values of an np.array.\n2484 |         DataFrame.round : Round values of a DataFrame.\n2485 | \n2486 |         Notes\n2487 |         -----\n2488 |         For values exactly halfway between rounded decimal values, pandas rounds\n2489 |         to the nearest even value (e.g. -0.5 and 0.5 round to 0.0, 1.5 and 2.5\n2490 |         round to 2.0, etc.).\n2491 | \n2492 |         Examples\n2493 |         --------\n2494 |         >>> s = pd.Series([-0.5, 0.1, 2.5, 1.3, 2.7])\n2495 |         >>> s.round()\n2496 |         0   -0.0\n2497 |         1    0.0\n2498 |         2    2.0\n2499 |         3    1.0\n2500 |         4    3.0\n2501 |         dtype: float64\n2502 |         \"\"\"\n2503 |         nv.validate_round(args, kwargs)\n2504 |         new_mgr = self._mgr.round(decimals=decimals)\n2505 |         return self._constructor_from_mgr(new_mgr, axes=new_mgr.axes).__finalize__(\n2506 |             self, method=\"round\"\n2507 |         )\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"dt.round\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.round`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n10822 | def round(\n10823 |         self, decimals: int | dict[IndexLabel, int] | Series = 0, *args, **kwargs\n10824 |     ) -> DataFrame:\n10825 |         \"\"\"\n10826 |         Round numeric columns in a DataFrame to a variable number of decimal places.\n10827 | \n10828 |         Parameters\n10829 |         ----------\n10830 |         decimals : int, dict, Series\n10831 |             Number of decimal places to round each column to. If an int is\n10832 |             given, round each column to the same number of places.\n10833 |             Otherwise dict and Series round to variable numbers of places.\n10834 |             Column names should be in the keys if `decimals` is a\n10835 |             dict-like, or in the index if `decimals` is a Series. Any\n10836 |             columns not included in `decimals` will be left as is. Elements\n10837 |             of `decimals` which are not columns of the input will be\n10838 |             ignored.\n10839 |         *args\n10840 |             Additional keywords have no effect but might be accepted for\n10841 |             compatibility with numpy.\n10842 |         **kwargs\n10843 |             Additional keywords have no effect but might be accepted for\n10844 |             compatibility with numpy.\n10845 | \n10846 |         Returns\n10847 |         -------\n10848 |         DataFrame\n10849 |             A DataFrame with the affected columns rounded to the specified\n10850 |             number of decimal places.\n10851 | \n10852 |         See Also\n10853 |         --------\n10854 |         numpy.around : Round a numpy array to the given number of decimals.\n10855 |         Series.round : Round a Series to the given number of decimals.\n10856 | \n10857 |         Notes\n10858 |         -----\n10859 |         For values exactly halfway between rounded decimal values, pandas rounds\n10860 |         to the nearest even value (e.g. -0.5 and 0.5 round to 0.0, 1.5 and 2.5\n10861 |         round to 2.0, etc.).\n10862 | \n10863 |         Examples\n10864 |         --------\n10865 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n10866 |         ...     [(0.21, 0.32), (0.01, 0.67), (0.66, 0.03), (0.21, 0.18)],\n10867 |         ...     columns=[\"dogs\", \"cats\"],\n10868 |         ... )\n10869 |         >>> df\n10870 |             dogs  cats\n10871 |         0  0.21  0.32\n10872 |         1  0.01  0.67\n10873 |         2  0.66  0.03\n10874 |         3  0.21  0.18\n10875 | \n10876 |         By providing an integer each column is rounded to the same number\n10877 |         of decimal places\n10878 | \n10879 |         >>> df.round(1)\n10880 |             dogs  cats\n10881 |         0   0.2   0.3\n10882 |         1   0.0   0.7\n10883 |         2   0.7   0.0\n10884 |         3   0.2   0.2\n10885 | \n10886 |         With a dict, the number of places for specific columns can be\n10887 |         specified with the column names as key and the number of decimal\n10888 |         places as value\n10889 | \n10890 |         >>> df.round({\"dogs\": 1, \"cats\": 0})\n10891 |             dogs  cats\n10892 |         0   0.2   0.0\n10893 |         1   0.0   1.0\n10894 |         2   0.7   0.0\n10895 |         3   0.2   0.0\n10896 | \n10897 |         Using a Series, the number of places for specific columns can be\n10898 |         specified with the column names as index and the number of\n10899 |         decimal places as value\n10900 | \n10901 |         >>> decimals = pd.Series([0, 1], index=[\"cats\", \"dogs\"])\n10902 |         >>> df.round(decimals)\n10903 |             dogs  cats\n10904 |         0   0.2   0.0\n10905 |         1   0.0   1.0\n10906 |         2   0.7   0.0\n10907 |         3   0.2   0.0\n10908 |         \"\"\"\n10909 |         from pandas.core.reshape.concat import concat\n10910 | \n10911 |         def _dict_round(df: DataFrame, decimals) -> Iterator[Series]:\n10912 |             for col, vals in df.items():\n10913 |                 try:\n10914 |                     yield _series_round(vals, decimals[col])\n10915 |                 except KeyError:\n10916 |                     yield vals\n10917 | \n10918 |         def _ser