example\n15 |     using the link at the bottom of the page.\n16 | \"\"\"\n17 | \n18 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n19 | import numpy as np\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/multicolored_line.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==================\n  3 | Multicolored lines\n  4 | ==================\n  5 | \n  6 | The example shows two ways to plot a line with the a varying color defined by\n  7 | a third value. The first example defines the color at each (x, y) point.\n  8 | The second example defines the color between pairs of points, so the length\n  9 | of the color value list is one less than the length of the x and y lists.\n 10 | \n 11 | Color values at points\n 12 | ----------------------\n 13 | \n 14 | \"\"\"\n 15 | \n 16 | import warnings\n 17 | \n 18 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 19 | import numpy as np\n 20 | \n 21 | from matplotlib.collections import LineCollection\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/event_handling/poly_editor.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==============\n  3 | Polygon editor\n  4 | ==============\n  5 | \n  6 | This is an example to show how to build cross-GUI applications using\n  7 | Matplotlib event handling to interact with objects on the canvas.\n  8 | \n  9 | .. note::\n 10 |     This example exercises the interactive capabilities of Matplotlib, and this\n 11 |     will not appear in the static documentation. Please run this code on your\n 12 |     machine to see the interactivity.\n 13 | \n 14 |     You can copy and paste individual parts, or download the entire example\n 15 |     using the link at the bottom of the page.\n 16 | \"\"\"\n 17 | \n 18 | import numpy as np\n 19 | \n 20 | from matplotlib.artist import Artist\n 21 | from matplotlib.lines import Line2D\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/event_handling/data_browser.